ESSAY PASSAGE — 'two layers, one tool' / strands vs skills.

Two instruments, one scarcity. Strands and skills both answer 'context is the
scarce resource' but attack different halves: a strand amortises STATE (don't
re-derive where you got to), a skill amortises PROCEDURE (don't re-derive how
it's done). A strand is a noun with history; a skill is a verb without one. If
it needs to know what happened last time it's a strand, not a skill.

The cost asymmetry is the argument: a skill loads ON DEMAND, so it costs nothing
in sessions that don't need it, whereas a strand's CLAUDE.md is paid in full at
every launch. Anything in an always-loaded file that only matters occasionally is
mis-filed — a procedure paying rent as state. Same push-cost-to-the-disposable-
place move as the main thesis, applied to INSTRUCTIONS rather than sessions.

The sharper version (Peter's observation 2026-08-16): a house tool is a TWO-LAYER
artifact — the deterministic layer (the script) and the judgement layer (when to
invoke it, in what order, what not to do instead). The house-tool convention is
therefore both a skill AND a source of skills. The skill is 'the residue that
doesn't fit in --help': a tool with good --help and no judgement around it needs
no skill; a tool whose real difficulty is knowing when and in what order needs
one. Test the ratio — dcp is nearly all judgement (super/bin/dcp is a one-line
echo stub, the entire procedure lived in GLOBAL.md prose), idea is nearly all
judgement (trivial tool, all 'when'), secrets is a substantial tool with one
hard-won rule.

Honest-evolution point (good for a hiring reader — reads as a real history, not a
retrofitted design story): the method grew a TOOL layer first, and the judgement
layer for those tools had nowhere to go until skills existed. So it got compressed
into one-line imperatives in GLOBAL.md, losing everything except the imperative —
paid for in every session, read in none. Corroborating evidence in the repo: the
aicli SessionStart hook's own comments say it exists because 'the CLAUDE.md ritual
prose kept getting forgotten'. That hook is a bespoke point-fix for exactly the
problem skills solve generally. Independent convergence on the same diagnosis is
strong material.

Also worth the CV angle: skills are Anthropic's own vocabulary, so 'I factored my
always-loaded context into on-demand skills' reads as fluency to a hiring reader,
where a bespoke 'strand' reads as idiosyncratic until explained. Having both, and
being able to say why each thing sits where it sits, IS the argument.

Built 2026-08-16 as first house skills: house-tools, dcp, strand-ideas (in
dotfiles/.claude/skills/). They are themselves usage-evidence for this passage.
